hello everyone  welcome to this tutorial on percentiles  and quartiles in statistics by simply  learn  in this tutorial we are going to learn  what is percentile and how we can  calculate the percentile  with the formula then we will move on to  qualitize and we'll see  what are qualities and how we can  calculate the quartiles with help of  formula  so without further ado let's get started  we'll start with percentiles so what  exactly is the percentile  a percentile is a term used in  statistics to express  how a score compares to other scores in  the same data set  while there is technically no standard  definition of percentile  it's typically communicated as a  percentage of values that fall below a  particular value  in a set of data scores percentage are  commonly used to report values from a  non-reference  test the formula to calculate a  percentile is px  is equal to x n plus 1 divided by 100  where x is the percentile you want to  calculate and n is the total number of  observations  let's understand this formula better  with the help of example  so how to calculate a percentile suppose  you have marks of 20 students  here we have the marks of 20 students 89  78 94  and so on the marks are scored out of  hundred  let's try to find out the 90th  percentile  so the first step to calculate the 90th  percentile is to sort the data in an  ascending order  so here we have sorted the data in  ascending order from 43 45 45  till 97.  the next step is to substitute the  values in the formula  so p90 will be equal to 90 20 plus 1  divided by 100  but 90 is the percentile you want to  calculate and 20 is the number of  observations  it should come to 1890 divided by 100  which will be approx to 94.  so our 90th percentile is a 94 marks in  the data set  what exactly does this 94 marks  signifies  p90 equal to 94 means that the 90 of the  students got less than 94 marks  and the 10 percent of the students got  the marks more than 94.  there's an another way to calculate the  percentile let's see how we can do that  suppose we want to calculate at what  percentile range the 78 marks fall  so to calculate this we have the formula  p is equal to small n divided by capital  n  into hundred where small n is equal to  ordinal rank of values and the capital n  is equal to total values in the data set  so substituting the values in the above  formula we get 12 into 100 divided by 20  which will be equal to 60 so this means  that the 78 mark signifies the 60th  percentile in the data set  now that we have discussed the  percentiles and know how to calculate  the percentile  let's move on to quartiles what are  quara tiles  quartile as a name suggest is a  statistical term that divides the data  into the four quarters  it basically divides the original data  points into the fourth quarters on a  number line  the q1 which is also called the first  quartile as the 25th percent of the data  set  the second quartile which is q2  signifies the 50 percent of the data set  and the third quartile which is q3  signifies 75  on the data set the q1 is also called a  lower quartile and upper quartile is  also called as q3  25 of all the data has a value  less than equal to q1 second quartile  which also the median  has 50 percent of all the data less than  m  75 of all the data has a value less than  equal to q2  and the 50 of all the data lies between  q1 and q3  if the measurement falls on the right  side of the upper quartile in a set of  data  then we know that it is in the top 25  percent of the data  if the measurement falls to the left of  the lower quartile of a set of data  then we know that it is in the bottom 25  of the data now that we have discussed  the theoretical aspect of quartiles  let's understand how we can calculate  the quartiles for this we have taken one  famous example let's consider that we  have the petal length of rose flowers  here we have the petal length in  centimeters to find the q1 q2 and q3  we first need to sort the data in  ascending order  so when you sort the data in the  ascending order we get the  following values 1.1.3 1.5  till 5.6 to find the m we have two  formulas  first when the n is odd and the second  when the n is even  since here the n is odd which is equal  to 15 will calculate the median by n  plus 1 by 2  which will be 16 by 2 which is equal to  the eighth value in the data set  so the q2 is the eighth observation  which is equal to 3.3 in the data set  to find the first quartile value we have  the formula q 1 is equal to n  plus 1 into 1 by 4 n plus 1 because we  have the  n as odd which is equal to 15. so  dividing 16 by  4 we get the value equal to 4. so q1 is  a 4th observation is equal to 2.0  so this point is our first quartile now  to calculate the q3 which is the top 25  percent of the data set  we multiply the n plus 1 into the 3x4  so q3 is the 12th observation which will  come out to be 4.2  so we have our q1 which is the first  quartile 2.0  q2 the second quartile which is also the  median at 3.3 and we have the q3 is  equal to third quartile  which is 4.2 so what does this point  signifies  one by four or twenty five percent of  the flowers have a petal length  that is less than or equal to two point  zero fifty percent of the flowers of the  petal length that is less than or equal  to three point three centimeter  three by four or seventy five percent of  the flowers have a petal length that is  less than or equal to 4.2  and half which is also 50 of the flowers  have a petal length between 2.6  centimeters and 4.2 centimeters  so with this we come to the end of this  tutorial on percentiles  and quartiles and statistics i hope you  like this video if yes please subscribe  our channel  and keep learning thank you  hi there if you like this video  subscribe to the simply learn youtube  channel  and click here to watch similar videos  turn it up and get certified  click here

